ven that $∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\text{ }i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\text{ }j.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445677 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "021b7b63-3816-5a7a-9f77-baf36bf193bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445677 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445678 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445678 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95051" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445678 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445678 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y)={x}^{4}-{y}^{4}$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445678 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "240fc49a-d022-5d2c-b6ad-58058500b98b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445678 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4})\\,j \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\,i-4{y}^{3}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =4{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,i-4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(4{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,i-4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t-4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445679 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445679 #edb/ref 1069 "q-167463" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445679 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445679 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)$ and $r(t)=t\\,i+2t\\,j+\\frac{1}{t}\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445679 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "430b6986-f30c-500a-b5e9-219dd0f3ace5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445679 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))\\,k \\\\  & =yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\,i+xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\,j+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,$ $y=2t$ and $z=\\frac{1}{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,j+2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+2\\,j-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,j+2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,k)\\cdot (\\,i+2\\,j-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot 2+2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445680 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445680 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36234" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445680 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445680 #edb/ref 1071 "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,2t⟩$ at the point $t=\\pi .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445680 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b98d6bad-fbc9-5978-8040-a1a35d995f54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445680 #edb/ref 1074 "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ and $y=2t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t},2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{1}{t},2⟩ \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{t}+2{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\pi ))] & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi }{\\pi }+2{ln}^{2}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi  \\\\  & =2{ln}^{2}⁡\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
